{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the user-only spec cumulatively, in order\nTreat each conversation as a running state update stream from **user messages only**. After every user shard, merge the new facts into the full accumulated specification and re-check the assistant against that updated state. Do not let assistant guesses, placeholders, or speculative completions become part of the task unless the user later confirms them.\n\n### 2) Recompute when later user facts may override earlier assumptions\nLater user turns are authoritative updates, not just additions. If a new message supplies a relation, quantity, outcome, or \u201cstill has / remaining / already available\u201d detail, re-evaluate the whole dependency chain from the updated spec. Many failures come from keeping an earlier interpretation alive after the user has effectively changed the target quantity.\n\n### 3) Judge against the full accumulated spec, not the latest turn in isolation\nA response can be locally reasonable on one turn and still be wrong relative to the complete user-derived state. Always compare the assistant\u2019s approach to the full accumulated specification at that point in the dialogue, including earlier totals, definitions, relations, and later refinements that may complete or override prior clues.\n\n### 4) Reassess completeness every time the user adds a shard\nEach new user message should trigger a fresh \u201cis the problem now fully determined?\u201d check. A task that was ambiguous earlier may become solvable later, so stale uncertainty should be retired when the latest shard closes the gap. Conversely, if a later shard introduces a decisive constraint, earlier partial conclusions may become obsolete.\n\n### 5) Treat \u201calready has / remaining / shared / total / each / per\u201d as high-signal scope changes\nAggregate language often changes the scale or structure of the answer. These cues should trigger a full re-check of quantities and totals, not a local patch. Similarly, relational phrasing such as comparisons, ratios, differences, remainders, complements, or \u201cthe rest\u201d should be translated into explicit dependencies and checked for actual use in the reasoning.\n\n### 6) Prefer update-and-recompute over preserving stale branches\nWhen new information arrives, keep only intermediate reasoning that still survives under the updated spec. Discard subtotals, assumptions, or partial conclusions whose inputs changed. If an earlier branch is no longer compatible with the latest user state, the analysis should identify that it must be replaced, not merely extended.\n\n### 7) Separate intermediate correctness from final-task correctness\nA derivation can be valid as an intermediate step while still failing the user\u2019s actual goal. Distinguish between:\n- whether the assistant got a useful partial relation right, and\n- whether it actually answered the full request under the final combined spec.\n\nThis prevents over-crediting answers that stop too early, solve only a subpart, or finalize before all constraints are in play.\n\n### 8) Detect premature closure and over-requesting in shard-based dialogues\nIf the assistant commits to an answer, model, or \u201cneed more info\u201d stance before the user has finished supplying constraints, flag that as premature closure. If the user later supplies the missing piece, the assistant must adapt immediately; lingering clarification-seeking after the task is determinate is a divergence, not caution. The analyzer should note the turn where the task becomes solvable and identify the first turn where the assistant should have switched from uncertainty to completion.\n\n### 9) Track arithmetic drift and stale derived quantities\nWhen new facts arrive, earlier derived values may become obsolete. Verify that the assistant propagated updated counts, totals, or rates consistently across the full conversation instead of reusing old subtotals after the spec changed. Also watch for cases where a derived quantity is carried too far\u2014such as treating a total as a per-unit value or vice versa\u2014without user support.\n\n### 10) Separate user-stated facts from assistant-invented scaffolding\nDo not reward decompositions, patterns, or hidden assumptions that were not supported by the user text. Track the type of each user fact\u2014direct value, multiplier, difference, remainder, definition, final-state condition, or cumulative update\u2014and verify that the assistant\u2019s reasoning is grounded in those facts rather than an invented subproblem or guessed pattern.\n\n### 11) Preserve valid intermediate facts, but strip stale uncertainty in compacted context\nIn rewritten or compacted context, carry forward the user facts and any correct derived relations that remain valid. Remove obsolete clarification paths, unsupported assumptions, and final-answer framing that no longer fits the updated spec. The goal is to start from the correct current state, not from an earlier interpretive dead end.\n\n### 12) In corrective feedback, name the missed constraint and its effect\nStrong \u201cWhat Needs to Change\u201d feedback identifies the exact user-stated fact or relation that was missed and explains how it changes the spec. Avoid vague advice; state what was omitted, why it matters, and what part of the reasoning must be re-evaluated. Only flag issues that affect correctness or task compliance\u2014don\u2019t overstate harmless omissions.\n\n### 13) Verify both answer correctness and output-form compliance\nThe analyzer should separately report whether the answer is correct under the full accumulated user spec and whether the assistant respected the required output form. A response may have the right value but still violate formatting or completion expectations, and a structurally plausible response may still miss the decisive final constraint.\n\n### 14) Use the latest shard to retire earlier objections\nIf the assistant previously said the problem was underdetermined, incomplete, or missing context, ask whether the latest user message resolves that gap. If it does, the earlier objection is no longer valid. The analysis should explicitly mark the point where the task becomes determined and avoid carrying forward stale uncertainty or outdated clarification requests.\n\n### 15) Prefer compact \u201cwhat looks right / what needs to change\u201d statements\nWhen giving feedback, isolate the first wrong assumption or missing update so the next response can recover without re-deriving the whole conversation. Compact diagnoses are especially effective in incremental disclosure settings because they preserve the correct cumulative state while removing only the outdated reasoning.",
  "version": 4,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build and continuously refresh a cumulative user-spec\nStart by extracting a **running specification from user turns only**. Treat each new user message as part of an accumulating constraint ledger, not as an isolated turn. Reconcile the latest user message against everything stated earlier, and update the spec immediately when a later turn supplies a missing parameter, relationship, or subgroup definition.  \nKey diagnostic: if a user has progressively revealed the full task, the analyzer should stop asking whether information is still missing and instead ask whether the latest disclosure changes the answer.\n\n### 2) Use the latest user disclosure as the highest-priority update\nWhen a later user message fills in a gap, it **supersedes placeholder assumptions** from earlier turns. Any earlier request for clarification, provisional formula, or tentative subtotal becomes non-binding once the needed value arrives.  \nWatch for cases where the assistant keeps reasoning from an earlier \u201cunknown\u201d state after the user has made the relevant quantity explicit. That is a common divergence pattern.\n\n### 3) Compare the assistant to the latest complete user-derived state, not the last turn alone\nThe analyzer should evaluate the assistant against the **full accumulated user state at that point**, not only against the immediately previous message. This is especially important in sharded or incremental disclosure conversations where the target quantity only becomes fully determined after several turns.  \nA response that is locally plausible on one shard can still be wrong relative to the final user specification.\n\n### 4) Re-evaluate the whole chain after each new shard\nDo not freeze on an earlier sufficient-looking answer. Each new user shard may add a constraint that changes the correct result, even if the earlier arithmetic was internally valid. The analyzer should re-check whether previous intermediate quantities still hold under the updated spec.  \nUseful signal: the assistant says a new fact \u201cdoesn\u2019t change the answer,\u201d but the new fact links to existing facts through a ratio, subset, remainder, or adjustment. That is often a true error, not a harmless commentary.\n\n### 5) Distinguish \u201cmissing information\u201d from \u201cnew information that changes the math\u201d\nA common failure mode is treating an earlier unknown as if it remains unknown even after the user provides it, or treating a newly revealed relationship as irrelevant when it actually constrains the solution space. The analyzer should explicitly decide whether the latest user turn merely completes the spec or materially revises it.  \nIf the user provides a total, percentage, ratio, subset count, remainder, or comparative relation, test whether it alters the target quantity before accepting any \u201cno effect\u201d claim.\n\n### 6) Prefer replacement-plus-recompute over preserving outdated branches\nWhen the user updates the problem, preserve only the intermediate results that remain valid under the new spec. Discard any subtotal, assumption, or decomposition whose inputs have changed. Then recompute from the updated cumulative spec.  \nThis matters especially when a later user message changes a category from \u201cunknown/inferable\u201d to \u201cknown aggregate\u201d or changes what group a percentage applies to. The old decomposition should not be allowed to anchor the next step.\n\n### 7) Do not invent decompositions or subproblems the user did not state\nFlag assistant reasoning that creates a breakdown not supported by the user\u2019s message. If the user gives an aggregate property for a subgroup, the analyzer should treat that aggregate directly rather than forcing it into invented subcomponents.  \nRelated pitfall: the assistant may introduce a plausible branch that was never required and then solve that branch correctly while missing the actual task. The analyzer should separate \u201clocally valid breakdown\u201d from \u201cuser-supported structure.\u201d\n\n### 8) Treat ratios, comparisons, and subset relations as real constraints\nUser statements that are not directly numeric still often imply concrete mathematical dependencies. The analyzer should convert relational language into explicit constraints and verify that the assistant used them.  \nTypical warning sign: the assistant acknowledges a relation such as \u201ctwice as many,\u201d \u201cone quarter of,\u201d or \u201cthe remaining subset,\u201d but then proceeds as though it does not affect the target quantity.\n\n### 9) Check for premature finalization\nFlag any answer that is confidently finalized before all user-provided constraints have been integrated. In incremental conversations, the assistant may solve an early subtask correctly but stop before carrying the remaining branches through to the final requested quantity.  \nSeparate \u201cintermediate quantities\u201d from the final output. A number can be correct as a partial result and still be wrong as the final answer.\n\n### 10) Diagnose arithmetic drift after updates\nWhen new facts arrive, verify that the assistant preserved earlier quantities correctly instead of silently reusing an outdated subtotal. This is a frequent source of error in multi-turn quantitative tasks: the arithmetic may be correct within one shard but inconsistent across the whole conversation.  \nLook for changed counts, totals, or rates that were not propagated into later recomputations.\n\n### 11) Preserve valid reasoning, but not invalid conclusions\nThe analyzer should keep \u201cwhat still works\u201d from earlier reasoning only if it remains valid under the latest spec. Prior correct steps should not be used to justify a final answer that no longer follows after later user disclosures.  \nIn S2-style edits, this means removing wrong-but-plausible intermediate reasoning once a later message invalidates it, so the next step starts from the correct baseline.\n\n### 12) Use compact corrective diagnostics\nPrefer concise \u201creplace-and-recompute\u201d feedback: identify which earlier assumptions are superseded, what new user fact changes the state, and what must be recomputed next. Avoid narrative restatements of already-solved steps.  \nThe most useful correction usually says: the earlier branch is obsolete, the new constraint must be folded into the running spec, and the final result should be recalculated from that updated state.\n\n### 13) In multi-entity problems, verify all branches are carried to completion\nIf the conversation involves multiple groups, entities, or parts, the analyzer should check that the assistant solved each relevant branch and then combined them correctly. A common miss is solving one branch cleanly while failing to carry the remaining branches through to the end.  \nAlso check for additive aggregation requirements at the end: when multiple updated components contribute to the final total, the assistant must recompute each part before summing.\n\n### 14) Keep the analysis grounded in user-stated facts only\nBuild the task spec from user messages, not from assistant speculation, and avoid letting provisional assistant assumptions contaminate the spec. The analyzer\u2019s job is to test the assistant against the user\u2019s evolving requirements, not to endorse the assistant\u2019s inferred structure unless the user has made it explicit or later confirmed it.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the task as an evolving user-only spec\nBuild the task spec from **user messages only**, and keep updating it after every shard. Treat each new user turn as a constraint update that may complete, refine, override, or expand the current spec. Do not let assistant assumptions enter the spec unless the user explicitly stated or later confirmed them.\n\n### 2) Judge against the full accumulated spec, not the latest turn in isolation\nAfter each new user disclosure, compare the assistant\u2019s response to the **entire user-derived state at that point**, not just the immediately preceding message. A reply can be locally reasonable on one shard and still be wrong relative to the final combined specification.\n\n### 3) Recompute the whole problem whenever a new constraint arrives\nDo not freeze on an earlier \u201cgood enough\u201d interpretation. When the user adds a total, multiplier, difference, subset relation, or any other linking fact, re-evaluate whether previous intermediate quantities still hold. The key question is not \u201cwas the old step valid then?\u201d but \u201cdoes it still satisfy the updated spec now?\u201d\n\n### 4) Treat scope modifiers as high-signal constraint changes\nWords and phrases like **each, per, for every, for each of N, his/her/their, remaining, shared equally, total, all together** often change the scale of the answer. These are not cosmetic details; they often require multiplying, aggregating, or reallocating earlier quantities. If the assistant ignores a late scope modifier, that is a strong divergence signal.\n\n### 5) Distinguish intermediate correctness from final correctness\nA derivation can be mathematically correct as a subtotal, partial relation, or intermediate bound while still failing the task because it stops too early. The analyzer should separately assess:\n- whether the assistant got a useful intermediate result right, and\n- whether it actually answered the user\u2019s full question.\n\n### 6) Flag premature closure and stale finalization\nIf the user has not finished revealing the problem, the assistant should not act as though the answer is final. Likewise, once later user facts complete the task, any earlier \u201cinsufficient information\u201d posture becomes obsolete. Watch for responses that finalize before all user-provided constraints are integrated or continue asking for missing information after the missing piece has arrived.\n\n### 7) Prefer \u201cupdate and recompute\u201d over preserving outdated branches\nWhen the user\u2019s later message changes the problem state, keep only the intermediate reasoning that remains valid under the updated spec. Discard subtotals, decompositions, or assumptions whose inputs changed. If a later fact makes an earlier branch stale, the analyzer should explicitly recognize that the branch must be replaced, not merely extended.\n\n### 8) Convert relational language into explicit constraints\nRatios, comparisons, subsets, remainders, \u201ctwice as many,\u201d \u201cone quarter of,\u201d \u201cthe rest,\u201d and similar relational statements often define the true structure of the answer. The analyzer should translate them into concrete dependencies and test whether the assistant used them. A common failure is acknowledging the relation but then solving as if it had no effect.\n\n### 9) Separate what the user actually stated from invented scaffolding\nDo not reward assistant-made decompositions or assumptions that are not supported by user text. The analysis should track the relationship type of each user fact \u2014 direct value, multiplier, difference, remainder, zero case, comparison \u2014 and verify that the assistant\u2019s reasoning is grounded in those facts rather than in an invented subproblem.\n\n### 10) Check for partial-solution anchoring\nA correct earlier subresult can mislead the assistant into stopping early or resisting later updates. The analyzer should flag cases where the assistant reuses a valid intermediate derivation but fails to incorporate a newer constraint that changes the final answer. Look especially for \u201cthat doesn\u2019t change the answer\u201d claims that are contradicted by a later link to totals, counts, or scope.\n\n### 11) Rebuild the complete contributing set before judging the total\nWhen the user\u2019s shards together imply one cumulative answer, verify the full set of contributing terms and make sure none are omitted. This is especially important in multi-entity conversations, where the assistant may solve one branch correctly but forget to carry the remaining branches through to the final aggregation.\n\n### 12) Detect arithmetic drift across updates\nWhen new facts arrive, earlier numbers can silently become obsolete. Check that the assistant propagated updated counts, totals, or rates consistently across the full conversation instead of reusing an old subtotal after the spec changed. This is a common source of \u201calmost right\u201d answers in incremental quantitative tasks.\n\n### 13) Use the latest user message as a completeness trigger\nEach new user shard should prompt a fresh completeness check: does this finally determine the answer, or does it merely add another piece? If it determines the answer, earlier uncertainty should be retired. If it expands the scope, earlier partial answers should be reclassified as incomplete rather than final.\n\n### 14) In corrective feedback, name the missed user constraint and the implication\nWhen explaining what needs to change, identify the exact user-stated fact or relation that was missed and state how it changes the spec. The strongest correction is compact and specific: it tells the analyzer what was omitted, why that omission matters, and what relationship must be re-evaluated next.\n\n### 15) Preserve valid reasoning, not stale conclusions\nIn S2-style rewrites or compacted context, carry forward the user facts and any correct intermediate derivations, but drop outdated final-answer framing, stale uncertainty, and invalid assumptions. The goal is to keep the baseline accurate so the next step starts from the updated specification, not from an obsolete interpretation.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the user-only spec cumulatively, turn by turn\nTreat the task as a stateful specification that grows only from **user messages**. After every shard, merge new user facts into the full accumulated spec and re-evaluate the assistant against that updated state. Do not let assistant guesses, placeholders, or speculative completions become part of the task unless the user later confirms them.\n\n### 2) Reassess completeness every time the user adds a shard\nEach new user message should trigger a fresh \u201cis the problem now fully determined?\u201d check. A conversation that was ambiguous earlier may become solvable later, so do not carry forward stale uncertainty. Likewise, if later user facts make the task more specific, earlier \u201cinsufficient information\u201d or clarification-seeking behavior can become obsolete and should be treated as such.\n\n### 3) Judge against the full accumulated spec, not the latest turn in isolation\nA response may be locally reasonable on one turn but wrong relative to the complete user-derived state. Always test the assistant\u2019s approach against the entire accumulated specification at that point in the dialogue, including earlier totals, relations, definitions, and later refinements that may override or complete previous clues.\n\n### 4) Treat later user facts as potential overrides, not just additions\nWhen the user introduces a new relation, quantity, definition, or final-state detail, check whether it resolves ambiguity or changes the meaning of earlier numbers. If it does, re-evaluate the causal chain rather than appending the fact to an old branch. This is especially important when a later shard supplies the minimal pivot fact that unlocks the solution.\n\n### 5) Prefer \u201cupdate and recompute\u201d over preserving stale branches\nWhen new information arrives, keep only intermediate reasoning that still survives under the updated spec. Discard subtotals, assumptions, or partial conclusions whose inputs changed. If an earlier branch becomes stale, the analysis should identify that it must be replaced, not merely extended.\n\n### 6) Distinguish full correctness from partially correct intermediate reasoning\nA derivation can be valid as an intermediate step, subtotal, or helper fact while still failing the user\u2019s actual goal. Separate:\n- whether the assistant got a useful intermediate relation right, and\n- whether it actually answered the full user request under the final combined spec.\n\nThis prevents over-crediting solutions that stop too early or finalize before all constraints are in play.\n\n### 7) Treat scope and aggregate language as high-signal constraint changes\nWords like **each, per, for every, remaining, shared equally, total, all together, over time, across turns** often change the scale or structure of the answer. These should trigger a full re-check of quantities and totals, not a local adjustment. Aggregate constraints are especially important because they can force a global balance relation that supersedes a previously plausible local interpretation.\n\n### 8) Convert relational language into explicit dependencies\nWhenever the user states comparisons, ratios, differences, remainders, complements, or \u201ctwice as many / one quarter / the rest\u201d style relations, translate them into concrete dependencies and check whether the assistant actually used them. A common failure mode is acknowledging the relation but then solving as if it had no effect. If the user gives a direct proportional or definitional relation, that relation should govern the interpretation.\n\n### 9) Separate user-stated facts from assistant-invented scaffolding\nDo not reward decompositions, patterns, or hidden assumptions that were not supported by the user text. Track the type of each user fact\u2014direct value, multiplier, difference, remainder, definition, or final-state condition\u2014and verify that the assistant\u2019s reasoning is grounded in those facts rather than in an invented subproblem or guessed pattern.\n\n### 10) Watch for premature closure and overconfident patterning\nIf the assistant commits to an answer, pattern, or model before the user has finished supplying constraints, flag that as premature closure. This includes treating a partial numeric interpretation as final, assuming a repeated pattern without evidence, or asking for missing information after the user has already provided enough to solve the problem. If a later shard resolves the missing piece, stale uncertainty should be retired.\n\n### 11) Check for arithmetic drift and overextended derived quantities\nWhen new facts arrive, earlier derived values may become obsolete. Verify that the assistant propagated updated counts, totals, or rates consistently across the full conversation instead of reusing old subtotals after the spec changed. Also watch for cases where a derived quantity is carried too far\u2014e.g., turning a total into a per-unit interpretation or vice versa\u2014without user support.\n\n### 12) Use the latest shard to test whether earlier objections still apply\nIf the assistant previously said the problem was underdetermined, incomplete, or missing context, ask whether the latest user message resolves that gap. If it does, the earlier objection is no longer valid. The analysis should explicitly mark the turn where the task becomes determined and identify the first turn where the assistant should have adapted.\n\n### 13) Preserve valid intermediate facts, but strip stale uncertainty in compacted context\nIn compacted or rewritten context, carry forward the user facts and any correct derived relations that remain valid. Remove obsolete clarification paths, unsupported assumptions, and final-answer framing that no longer fits the updated spec. The goal is to start from the correct state, not from an earlier interpretive dead end.\n\n### 14) When giving corrective feedback, name the missed constraint and its effect\nThe strongest \u201cWhat Needs to Change\u201d feedback identifies the exact user-stated fact or relation that was missed and explains how it changes the spec. Avoid vague advice; state what was omitted, why it matters, and what aspect of the reasoning must be re-evaluated. Only flag issues that actually affect correctness or task compliance\u2014don\u2019t overstate harmless omissions if they do not change the solved value.\n\n### 15) Separate solution correctness from spec completeness\nA final answer can be numerically right while still failing to incorporate all later user details, and a response can be structurally plausible while missing the decisive final constraint. The analyzer should report both dimensions precisely: whether the answer is correct under the full accumulated user spec, and whether the assistant\u2019s process respected the evolving completeness of that spec."
